<p>Writing to Robert Gathorne-Hardy in 1929, Logan Pearsall Smith referred to an account in the London <em>Times</em> of a solar eclipse as seen from Manila:</p>
<blockquote><p>
The weather was cloudless, the conditions were perfect. Acacia trees closed their leaves as for the night; dew fell, chickens roosted, and the peasants in outlying villages, terrified by the awful phenomenon, supplicated the Saints.
</p></blockquote>
<p>Smith wrote, &#8220;Could this sentence, which Flaubert might have written with the help of Gibbon, be the chance product of a journalist&#8217;s pen? I cannot think so.&#8221;</p>
<p><a href="https://archive.org/details/lastcuckooverybe0000unse/page/18/mode/2up">For the record</a>, a <em>Times</em> archivist attributed the passage to Willmott Lewis, who was Washington correspondent for the paper from 1920 to 1948.</p>
